  ],
  "opening": "Chapter 5 followed a single qubit from input through unitary gates to measurement. A computation with several qubits follows the same rules, but its amplitudes belong to complete bit strings. How do we operate on one part of such a register, couple its parts, and read out an answer?",
  "opening_continuation": [
    "We begin with the joint-state rule from Chapter 4 and turn it into working circuit notation. Local gates update individual labels; controlled gates connect them. A two-qubit computation leads to entanglement, and measurements show why joint amplitudes contain more information than separate output counts.",
    "We then examine the limits on copying and signalling, and work through teleportation as a complete three-qubit computation with measurement and classical control. Chapter 7 will use this machinery for function evaluation and phase kickback."
  ]
